<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liu Ziye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">449–466</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Qianfei of Liu Song</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liu Ziye, known as the Former Deposed Emperor (Qianfei), was the sixth emperor of the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, reigning from 464 to 466. Succeeding his father Emperor Xiaowu as a teenager, he became notorious for cruelty, killing senior ministers and many of his own relatives. He was assassinated in a palace plot and succeeded by his uncle, Emperor Ming.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Liu Ziye, childhood name Fashi, was born on 25 February 449, when his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaowu-of-liu-song">Liu Jun</a> was Prince of Wuling under Emperor Wen. He stayed at the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> and was imprisoned by his uncle Liu Shao, who murdered Emperor Wen and seized the throne in 453; once Liu Jun had defeated Liu Shao and become Emperor Xiaowu, Liu Ziye was freed and made crown prince on 13 March 454. Often rebuked by his father, he resented both him and his brother Liu Ziluan, whom the emperor at times thought of making heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He came to the throne in July 464 and at once rescinded his father's changes to the laws of Emperor Wen. When his mother, Empress Dowager Wang, fell gravely ill late that year, he refused to visit her. At first power lay with his father's associates Dai Faxing and Chao Shangzhi, but in the autumn of 465 he forced Dai to kill himself. When Liu Yuanjing and Yan Shibo plotted to put his granduncle Liu Yigong, Prince of Jiangxia, on the throne, the general Shen Qingzhi exposed them, and the emperor led the guards in killing Liu Yigong, his four sons and the plotters' families.</p><p>He forced Liu Ziluan to kill himself and had two of his brother's siblings put to death. When his aunt's husband He Mai planned to replace him with his brother Liu Zixun, the emperor killed He Mai, and when Shen Qingzhi urged him to change his ways, he poisoned him. He kept his uncles under house arrest in the palace, beat them, and caged the heavier ones to be weighed like pigs; Liu Xiuren, Prince of Jian'an, saved Liu Yu more than once by flattery. An order to poison Liu Zixun at Xunyang leaked and gave his aide Deng Wan the chance to rise in revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After he had a lady-in-waiting beheaded because she looked like a woman who had cursed him in a dream, he held a ceremony to kill ghosts. There his attendant Shou Jizhi, often rebuked by him, sprang a plot with other conspirators and killed him, on 1 January 466. He was buried with his first wife, He Lingwan, and his uncle Liu Yu took the throne as Emperor Ming. His reign of less than eighteen months is remembered for its slaughter of high officials and for conduct his contemporaries thought deeply immoral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liu Ziye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Xiaowu's son <b>Liu Ziye</b>, who succeeded in 464, is generally regarded as a tyrant who put several of his uncles to death; one of those uncles assassinated him and became Emperor Ming.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liu-song">Liu Song · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
